1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,875
Have you ever wondered, if I run this

2
00:00:01,875 --> 00:00:04,083
code twice, will I get a different answer,

3
00:00:04,083 --> 00:00:05,917
since it's supposed to be random?

4
00:00:06,167 --> 00:00:09,000
Here's the short version: a seed doesn't decide

5
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:11,792
whether an answer is true. All it decides

6
00:00:11,792 --> 00:00:14,208
is whether a random process can be repeated

7
00:00:14,458 --> 00:00:16,000
— so you can run it again and

8
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:19,250
inspect exactly the same thing. Repeatable is

9
00:00:19,250 --> 00:00:21,750
not the same as true, and that distinction

10
00:00:21,750 --> 00:00:23,375
is the whole point of this video.

11
00:00:23,625 --> 00:00:25,958
So what's actually happening in this script? It

12
00:00:25,958 --> 00:00:29,333
predicts three outcomes — roughly a 9% chance,

13
00:00:29,667 --> 00:00:33,917
a 24.5% chance, and a 66.5%

14
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:37,250
chance. Then it goes one step further: it

15
00:00:37,250 --> 00:00:40,583
simulates 1,000 draws from that distribution, using

16
00:00:40,583 --> 00:00:42,542
a fixed starting number called a seed.

17
00:00:42,792 --> 00:00:46,667
Let's actually test it. I ran main.py, unmodified,

18
00:00:46,667 --> 00:00:48,833
with seed 7 — twice, back to back.

19
00:00:49,375 --> 00:00:51,417
Both runs came back with the exact same

20
00:00:51,417 --> 00:00:55,333
probabilities: 0.0900,

21
00:00:55,542 --> 00:00:58,917
0.2447, 0.6652.

22
00:00:59,125 --> 00:01:02,292
And the same counts too — 102, 268,

23
00:01:02,292 --> 00:01:05,583
630. Not close. Identical,

24
00:01:05,583 --> 00:01:06,750
down to the last digit.

25
00:01:07,042 --> 00:01:08,958
Now watch what happens if I change only

26
00:01:08,958 --> 00:01:10,833
one thing — the seed, from 7 to

27
00:01:10,833 --> 00:01:15,375
99. The result shifts: 93, 267,

28
00:01:15,625 --> 00:01:19,167
640. Same process. Different seed.

29
00:01:19,792 --> 00:01:22,875
Different result. That's the whole mechanism, laid bare

30
00:01:22,875 --> 00:01:25,167
— no mystery, just a fixed starting point

31
00:01:25,167 --> 00:01:26,625
for a random number generator.

32
00:01:26,958 --> 00:01:30,583
Same starting point. Different seed. Different path. Different

33
00:01:30,583 --> 00:01:31,125
destination.

34
00:01:31,583 --> 00:01:34,500
There's one more distinction worth making. A seed

35
00:01:34,500 --> 00:01:37,083
does not remove randomness from the model. It

36
00:01:37,083 --> 00:01:39,458
gives the random number generator a fixed place

37
00:01:39,458 --> 00:01:42,500
to begin. From that starting point, the same

38
00:01:42,500 --> 00:01:46,375
sequence of pseudo-random choices is produced again. Change

39
00:01:46,375 --> 00:01:49,500
the starting point, and the sequence changes, even

40
00:01:49,500 --> 00:01:52,083
though the code, the probabilities, and the number

41
00:01:52,083 --> 00:01:54,458
of draws all stay the same. That is

42
00:01:54,458 --> 00:01:57,667
why seed seven and seed ninety-nine can produce

43
00:01:57,667 --> 00:02:01,458
different counts without representing different experiments. They

44
00:02:01,500 --> 00:02:04,000
are two repeatable runs of the same experiment,

45
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:07,208
with different starting points. Reproducibility

46
00:02:07,208 --> 00:02:10,250
tells us what happened under those conditions. It

47
00:02:10,250 --> 00:02:12,417
does not tell us that those conditions produced

48
00:02:12,417 --> 00:02:13,375
the right answer.

49
00:02:13,625 --> 00:02:15,875
Here's where people get tripped up. If an

50
00:02:15,875 --> 00:02:18,125
AI gives you a wrong answer, and you

51
00:02:18,125 --> 00:02:19,708
fix the seed, you don't get a better

52
00:02:19,708 --> 00:02:22,000
answer — you get that exact same wrong

53
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:25,875
answer, perfectly, every time. Reproducing

54
00:02:25,875 --> 00:02:28,708
a mistake with total consistency doesn't fix the

55
00:02:28,708 --> 00:02:32,042
mistake. It just makes it very, very repeatable.

56
00:02:32,208 --> 00:02:34,750
So remember the boundary: this shows you the

57
00:02:34,750 --> 00:02:37,708
process is repeatable. It tells you nothing about

58
00:02:37,708 --> 00:02:40,958
whether any single output is correct, safe, or

59
00:02:40,958 --> 00:02:44,208
good. Those are two different questions, and it's

60
00:02:44,208 --> 00:02:46,333
easy to quietly swap one for the other.

61
00:02:46,542 --> 00:02:48,958
Your turn: take any seeded code you've got,

62
00:02:49,167 --> 00:02:51,667
run it twice, and check, is it identical?

63
00:02:52,292 --> 00:02:55,375
Then change only the seed and compare. One

64
00:02:55,375 --> 00:02:58,125
small experiment, and you'll feel the difference between

65
00:02:58,125 --> 00:02:59,958
repeatable and true for yourself.

66
00:03:00,292 --> 00:03:01,333
Repeatable isn't true.
